#!/bin/sh
# mux-env_lib: the session pointers a pane must not start without.
#
# THE BUG THIS EXISTS FOR, measured rather than imagined. Every agent pane on
# a machine was running with no `SSH_AUTH_SOCK`: `git push` failed in every
# repo and `git fetch` in the four with ssh origins, while the box was healthy
# (the agent held the key and could sign, an https fetch worked with no key).
# It read as broken configuration and it was a missing variable.
#
# WHY IT IS MUX'S TO OWN: A PANE'S ENVIRONMENT COMES FROM THE SERVER, not from
# whoever asked for the pane. Measured, a client holding a perfectly good
# value still got the SERVER's value in a new window, so no caller can fix
# this from outside however careful it is.
#
# AND AN ATTACH DESTROYS, which is the actual mechanism and worse than a gap.
# tmux's `update-environment` copies the attaching client's environment into
# the SESSION, and copying an ABSENT variable records a REMOVAL:
#
#     session before : SSH_AUTH_SOCK=/tmp/GOOD
#     after attach   : -SSH_AUTH_SOCK
#     pane after     : UNSET
#
# One attach from a client with no agent turns a healthy session broken, and a
# session-level removal MASKS the server global, so there is no lower layer to
# fall back on. Measured both ways. On the box it was found on, the human was
# latched in from ANOTHER MACHINE while that box sat at its login greeter, so
# the attaching client was an agent-less ssh. Any cross-box attach does it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THREE TIERS, AND ONLY THE MIDDLE ONE IS MUX'S
#
#   NAMES       `envhooks.d/<VARNAME>`, one file per variable. The directory
#               LISTING is the set, so no separate list can drift from the
#               validators and `ls` answers "what does mux manage here".
#               WHICH variables matter is not mux's knowledge: this fleet
#               needs four and another environment needs others, so mux
#               holding the list makes every such environment a patch to mux.
#   VALUES      mux's own generic hunt (`mux_env_resolve`): the systemd user
#               manager first, then the caller's own environment.
#   VALIDATION  tier 1's hook, run with the value as $1. NOT mux's, because
#               the value is usually not the thing to check.
#
# WHY VALIDATION CANNOT BE A CLOSED VOCABULARY, the one design point worth
# reading twice. Of the five real variables only TWO validate directly:
#
#     SSH_AUTH_SOCK    /run/user/1000/gcr/ssh    the value itself
#     WAYFIRE_SOCKET   /run/user/1000/wayfire-wayland-1-.socket  likewise
#     WAYLAND_DISPLAY  wayland-1                 $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/$v
#     DBUS_..._ADDRESS unix:path=/run/user/.../bus  its unix:path= field
#     DISPLAY          :0                        /tmp/.X11-unix/X0
#
# VALIDATING DIRECTLY IS NOT VALIDATING CHEAPLY, which the two path-shaped
# ones disagree about: SSH_AUTH_SOCK stops at presence on purpose, while
# WAYFIRE_SOCKET must CONNECT, because a wayfire socket is not removed when
# its compositor exits and an agent's is. Read both before copying either.
#
# Three of five need a TRANSFORM before anything can be checked, and two of
# those transforms need ANOTHER variable's resolved value, which a vocabulary
# of kinds (socket, file, dir) cannot express. So every name is resolved
# BEFORE any is validated, and the resolved set reaches each hook under a
# `MUX_RESOLVED_` prefix: no numbering, no dependency declarations, and the
# prefix keeps a possibly-dead candidate out of the hook's own environment.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE IS NO CACHE, and that is a decision rather than an omission.
#
# The standing rule is SESSIONS COME AND GO AND MUX LIVES ON, and the numbers
# are decisive. Measured on the box above:
#
#     tmux server started      22:14:22
#     graphical session ENDED  22:30:32      <- sixteen minutes later
#     a pane in it             next day
#
# THE SERVER OUTLIVED THE THING ITS POINTERS POINT AT BY NINETEEN HOURS, and
# the dead session left its socket FILE behind so a presence test still
# answered yes. Any value cached at server start was stale within sixteen
# minutes. A cache is precisely the thing that outlives the session that
# filled it, so mux re-derives and re-validates every time; the hunt costs
# about 5ms, not worth optimising into a staleness bug.
#
# AND A PRESENCE TEST ON A HANDLE IS NOT A LIVENESS TEST, learned the hard way
# here: an earlier repair validated WAYLAND_DISPLAY with
# `[ -e "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/$v" ]` and it PASSED on a socket whose listener had
# been gone a day. A unix socket outlives its listener. Hence validation is
# the hook's job rather than a rule mux writes down: only the hook knows that
# this one needs a connect.

# --- WHICH NAMES -----------------------------------------------------------
# UNION WITH PER-NAME OVERRIDE, the overlay order layouts, themes and the
# latch hooks already use: $MUX_DIR wins over $MUX_SHARE for the SAME name,
# and a name in only one of them still counts. So mux ships a working set and
# a user replaces one or adds their own, neither side knowing about the other.
#
# SHIPPED ACTIVE rather than as an unwired library, the one place where "a
# shipped default that fails is worse than no default" does NOT apply. That
# rule came from shipping a LOCATION (`scan ~/src 3`), a claim about a
# particular machine, which made `mux check` fail on every box that keeps work
# elsewhere. A validator is not a location: with no ssh agent,
# `envhooks.d/SSH_AUTH_SOCK` finds nothing and mux does nothing, silently and
# correctly. Shipping them UNWIRED would mean a fresh install still starts
# panes with no agent, the whole bug this file exists for.
#
# KNOWN GAP, recorded rather than designed around: no way to opt OUT of a
# shipped name. R3 asks for the set to be EXTENSIBLE and says nothing about
# shrinking it, and a harm is hard to name, since R9 below means a session
# already holding a working value keeps it. If a reason appears, the mechanism
# wants thinking about rather than guessing at now.
mux_env_names() {
  for _end in "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d" \
              "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d/strip.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d/strip.d"; do
    [ -d "$_end" ] || continue
    for _enf in "$_end"/*; do
      # `-f` is what keeps `strip.d` itself out of the name list: a
      # directory is not a file, so it cannot be read as a variable.
      [ -f "$_enf" ] || continue
      printf '%s\n' "${_enf##*/}"
    done
  done | LC_ALL=C sort -u
}

# --- DECLARED REMOVAL: envhooks.d/strip.d/<VARNAME> ------------------------
# THE USER'S DESIGN (2026-09-30), and the argument is not that it adds a
# capability: stripping was ALREADY possible, since a validator exiting 1
# makes the value invalid and invalid already means "do not write it, clear it
# if present". Verified before this was built.
#
# WHAT IT ADDS IS POLICY VERSUS DIAGNOSIS, which matters because of what mux
# does with each. A validator saying 1 means THIS VALUE IS BROKEN, so mux acts
# and says so loudly, every time, per R7. A deliberate opt-out expressed that
# way would announce `dropped WAYLAND_DISPLAY` on every run for ever, noise
# about a decision the human already made. A declared strip is not a finding:
# the same shape as "I could not answer" and "the answer is no" being
# different claims.
#
# THE SAME SHAPE AS A VALIDATOR, deliberately: one file per variable, the
# directory listing is the set, $MUX_DIR overrides $MUX_SHARE per name. Anyone
# who understands envhooks.d understands this untold.
#
# IT SEES THE VALUE, the half that makes it more than a list of names:
#
#     exit 0                              always; I manage this myself
#     case $1 in /run/user/999/*) exit 0  only values from a dead runtime dir
#                ;; *) exit 1 ;; esac
#
# 0 MEANS STRIP, because the hook answers "should this be removed?" and true
# is yes. That is the opposite of the validator next door, where 1 means the
# value is bad, and the asymmetry is deliberate: a `strip.d` file whose "no"
# meant "strip it" would be a trap for whoever writes the second one.
#
# AND ANYTHING ELSE MEANS KEEP, the only safe direction for a hook whose yes
# deletes data. A missing hook, one that errors, one that times out: none has
# made a finding, and the validators' three-answer discipline says a
# non-finding must not be acted on. Here, never delete on doubt.
mux_env_strip_hook() {   # <VARNAME>
  for _esd in "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d/strip.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d/strip.d"; do
    if [ -f "$_esd/$1" ]; then printf '%s' "$_esd/$1"; return 0; fi
  done
  return 1
}

# True when this name/value pair is declared strippable. Bounded and with
# stdin closed for mux_env_validate's reasons: a user-supplied hook's cost is
# unbounded and this runs on the path a human types most.
mux_env_strips() {   # <VARNAME> <value> -> 0 strip, non-zero keep
  _esh=$(mux_env_strip_hook "$1") || return 1
  [ -x "$_esh" ] || return 1
  _est=$(mux_env_timeout)
  # THE if/else IS THE RETURN VALUE: whichever branch ran supplies the
  # status, so there is nothing to test afterwards. Only an exact 0 strips;
  # 124 from `timeout`, 126/127 from an unrunnable hook and anything else
  # mean no finding was made, so the value is kept.
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    timeout "$_est" "$_esh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  else
    "$_esh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  fi
}

# The hook for one name, $MUX_DIR first. Empty when there is none, which
# cannot happen for a name that came out of mux_env_names but can for one a
# caller asked about directly.
mux_env_hook() {   # <VARNAME>
  for _ehd in "$MUX_DIR/envhooks.d" "$MUX_SHARE/envhooks.d"; do
    if [ -f "$_ehd/$1" ]; then printf '%s' "$_ehd/$1"; return 0; fi
  done
  return 1
}

# --- TIER 2: THE VALUE -----------------------------------------------------
# THE SYSTEMD USER MANAGER FIRST, because it was right on both boxes
# including the broken one, where the correct values sat one command away the
# whole time while the tmux server, the window watcher and every pane were
# wrong. It also OUTLIVES the graphical session and the tmux server, which is
# what the no-cache argument above turns on.
#
# THEN THE CALLER'S OWN ENVIRONMENT, covering a box with no user manager: if
# the shell running mux has a working value that beats nothing. Second,
# because a long-lived shell can itself be stale.
#
# ONE CALL FOR THE WHOLE SET, not one per name. Measured: the hunt is about
# 5ms, and a naive one-read-per-variable cascade was 31ms.
mux_env_resolve() {   # -> VARNAME=value lines, for the names mux manages
  _erset=$(systemctl --user show-environment 2>/dev/null || true)
  mux_env_names | while IFS= read -r _ern; do
    [ -n "$_ern" ] || continue
    # Anchored, so SSH_AUTH_SOCK cannot be matched by a name merely
    # ENDING with it.
    _erv=$(printf '%s\n' "$_erset" | sed -n "s/^$_ern=//p" | head -1)
    if [ -z "$_erv" ]; then
      # The caller's own environment, read without eval: a variable name is
      # not a command and must never be treated as one.
      _erv=$(env | sed -n "s/^$_ern=//p" | head -1)
    fi
    [ -n "$_erv" ] || continue
    printf '%s=%s\n' "$_ern" "$_erv"
  done
}

# --- TIER 3: VALIDATION, WITH THREE ANSWERS --------------------------------
# THREE OUTCOMES, NOT TWO, and the third one is the whole point:
#
#     0        valid        use it
#     1        invalid      it is dead, do not write it
#     other    UNDETERMINED pass the value through unchanged
#
# latch's 0/1/78 shape, for the reason recorded there: a hook that is
# MISSING, ERRORS, or RUNS OUT OF TIME has made no finding, and reading that
# as "found it dead" is the very sin this file exists to fix, committed by a
# different mechanism.
#
# NOTHING HERE MAY HANG, because this runs on the path a human types most. A
# user-supplied validator's cost is unbounded and not mux's to predict, and
# this is not theoretical: the session that produced this design killed a
# background job hung FOURTEEN HOURS on a command blocking for input on a
# stdin that never reached EOF. For a session launcher that is the worst
# available failure, since the tool you reach for to fix things is the one
# that will not start.
#
# `< /dev/null` IS AS LOAD-BEARING AS THE TIMEOUT. A hook reading stdin would
# otherwise consume the caller's, and with no terminal block for ever in
# exactly that shape. The timeout bounds it; closing stdin means it never
# starts.
#
# THE BOUND IS A SEAM, environment then config then default, latch's shape,
# because an unusual environment may pay a second or two of startup for a
# validator with real work to do. Default 2s: long enough for a connect to a
# local socket, short enough that a wedged hook is not a wedged mux.
MUX_ENV_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT=2

# latch's `_seam` read (environment, $MUX_DIR/config, default), spelled out
# here rather than shared because latch's copy is a local function in a
# command and this is a lib. A NON-NUMERIC value is treated as ABSENT rather
# than handed to `timeout`, which would fail per hook and turn every answer
# into "undetermined": a config typo must not silently disable validation.
mux_env_timeout() {
  _ett=${MUX_ENV_TIMEOUT:-}
  if [ -z "$_ett" ] && [ -r "$MUX_DIR/config" ]; then
    _ett=$(mux_conf_clean 2>/dev/null <"$MUX_DIR/config" \
      | awk '$1 == "env-timeout" { print $2; exit }')
  fi
  case ${_ett:-} in
  ''|*[!0-9]*) printf '%s' "$MUX_ENV_TIMEOUT_DEFAULT" ;;
  *) printf '%s' "$_ett" ;;
  esac
}

# Validate one already-resolved value. $2 is the value; the caller must have
# exported the resolved set already (see mux_env_plan).
#
# NO `timeout` ON THE BOX IS NOT AN ERROR, just one fewer guarantee: the hook
# still runs and answers. mux only calls `timeout` behind `command -v`,
# because it is a harness dependency here and must not become a product one.
mux_env_validate() {   # <VARNAME> <value> -> 0 valid, 1 invalid, 2 undetermined
  _evh=$(mux_env_hook "$1") || return 2
  [ -x "$_evh" ] || return 2
  _evt=$(mux_env_timeout)
  if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    timeout "$_evt" "$_evh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  else
    "$_evh" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  fi
  _evr=$?
  # `timeout` reports 124 for a hook that ran out of time, which is
  # "made no finding" and must not read as invalid.
  case $_evr in
  0) return 0 ;;
  1) return 1 ;;
  *) return 2 ;;
  esac
}

# --- THE PLAN --------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolve everything, validate everything, then say what to DO. Emitting a
# plan rather than acting is what lets `mux check` report the same answer this
# would apply without applying it: R7 asks for a withheld value to be visible,
# and a reporter that re-derives the decision is a second implementation free
# to disagree with the first.
#
# Lines are `<VERDICT> <VARNAME> <value>`, value possibly empty:
#
#     set    NAME value   resolved and valid
#     keep   NAME value   resolved, and the hook could not tell (R5)
#     drop   NAME         resolved and INVALID: do not write, clear if present
#     none   NAME         nothing resolved at all
mux_env_plan() {
  # EXPORTED BEFORE ANY VALIDATION, which removes the ordering problem:
  # WAYLAND_DISPLAY's hook needs XDG_RUNTIME_DIR's resolved value, and
  # neither has to declare it or run in a particular order.
  _epres=$(mux_env_resolve)
  printf '%s\n' "$_epres" | while IFS= read -r _epl; do
    [ -n "$_epl" ] || continue
    export "MUX_RESOLVED_${_epl%%=*}=${_epl#*=}"
  done
  # A second pass, because the exports above are in a pipeline subshell and
  # would not reach the validation below. A here-doc, so a value containing
  # whitespace survives and nothing is word-split.
  while IFS= read -r _epl; do
    [ -n "$_epl" ] || continue
    export "MUX_RESOLVED_${_epl%%=*}=${_epl#*=}"
  done <<EOF
$_epres
EOF

  mux_env_names | while IFS= read -r _epn; do
    [ -n "$_epn" ] || continue
    _epv=$(printf '%s\n' "$_epres" | sed -n "s/^$_epn=//p" | head -1)
    # STRIP IS ASKED FIRST, about whichever value is in play: here the
    # RESOLVED one, deciding whether to WRITE it, while mux_env_apply asks
    # again about the SESSION's own value, deciding whether to REMOVE that.
    # One question, two subjects, which is why the hook takes the value and
    # not only the name.
    #
    # ASKED EVEN WHEN NOTHING RESOLVED, because "mux, remove this" must work
    # for a name with no value to find: the whole point may be that the
    # session holds something the login session does not.
    if mux_env_strips "$_epn" "$_epv"; then
      printf 'strip %s\n' "$_epn"; continue
    fi
    if [ -z "$_epv" ]; then printf 'none %s\n' "$_epn"; continue; fi
    # `|| _epvr=$?` AND NOT A BARE CALL, and not style: under `set -e` a
    # command returning non-zero outside a condition context KILLS the
    # shell, and this loop runs inside `$( )` in callers that all set -eu. A
    # bare call TRUNCATED THE PLAN at the first INVALID name, silently, so
    # every name after it alphabetically went unconsidered. Measured: four
    # names without `set -e`, two with it.
    #
    # Caught by `mux check` reporting [OK] about a pointer it should have
    # warned on, one day after that marker was added.
    _epvr=0
    mux_env_validate "$_epn" "$_epv" || _epvr=$?
    case $_epvr in
    0) printf 'set %s %s\n'  "$_epn" "$_epv" ;;
    1) printf 'drop %s\n'    "$_epn" ;;
    *) printf 'keep %s %s\n' "$_epn" "$_epv" ;;
    esac
  done
}

# --- APPLYING IT: ensure a VALID value, never replace a working one --------
# R9, and NOT "make every session agree". Converging every session onto one
# answer makes two sessions with deliberately DIFFERENT environments
# impossible: they collapse into each other, silently. The four variables that
# prompted this are box-wide pointers, identical per session by nature, so
# nothing would break today, but the set is extensible ON PURPOSE and the
# first genuinely per-session variable somebody adds would. So the job is STOP
# A PANE STARTING BROKEN, not enforce uniformity.
#
#     session has no value, or -NAME  -> set it      (the poisoned session)
#     session's value VALIDATES       -> leave alone (someone meant that)
#     session's value FAILS           -> replace it  (the stale pointer)
#     session's value UNDETERMINED    -> leave alone (R5: no finding made)
#
# THE SESSION'S OWN VALUE IS VALIDATED TOO, not just the one mux resolved,
# which is what makes the table above more than decoration. It also means a
# `drop` in the plan does NOT automatically clear the session: the login
# session's pointer can be dead while one session holds a working one.
#
# `-u` AND NEVER `-r` when clearing. `-r` is tmux's REMOVAL marker, the very
# `-NAME` that masks the global and started this investigation, so clearing
# with it reintroduces the bug in the name of fixing it. `-u` clears cleanly.
#
# EVERY DECISION IS ANNOUNCED ON REQUEST, per R7. Silence would make this a
# SECOND silent removal mechanism beside `update-environment`, which is what
# made the original fault cost a long session to find: everything reported
# healthy. The caller decides where that goes; this says what it did.
#
# `-n` REPORTS WITHOUT ACTING, and has to live HERE rather than in the caller.
# The first version had `mux update-env -n` print mux_env_plan, and the plan
# says what RESOLVED and validated while the decision to write also consults
# the SESSION's existing value: the dry run listed two names and the real run
# changed three. A preview computed by a different code path from the action
# is not a preview, and the comment claiming otherwise was the giveaway.
mux_env_apply() {   # <session-or-empty> [-v|-n] -> what it changed/would
  _eas=${1:-}
  _eav=${2:-}
  _ean=0
  [ "$_eav" != -n ] || { _ean=1; _eav=-v; }
  mux_env_plan | while IFS=' ' read -r _eaverd _eaname _eaval; do
    [ -n "$_eaname" ] || continue
    # What this session holds now. tmux answers three ways, and they are
    # three different facts: `NAME=value`, `-NAME` (REMOVED, the one that
    # matters most), or an error for a name it never heard of.
    if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
      _eacur=$(tm show-environment -t "$_eas" "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true)
    else
      _eacur=$(tm show-environment -g "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true)
    fi
    case $_eacur in
    "$_eaname="*) _eahave=${_eacur#"$_eaname="} ;;
    *)            _eahave= ;;
    esac

    # A DECLARED STRIP IS ANSWERED BEFORE VALIDITY, being a different kind
    # of statement: not "is this good" but "I do not want this here". Asked
    # about the SESSION's value, since that is what would be removed, so a
    # conditional hook can strip one value and leave another.
    if [ "$_eaverd" = strip ] || \
       { [ -n "$_eahave" ] && mux_env_strips "$_eaname" "$_eahave"; }; then
      if [ -n "$_eahave" ]; then
        if [ "$_ean" -eq 0 ]; then
          if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
            tm set-environment -t "$_eas" -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          else
            tm set-environment -g -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          fi
        fi
        # `stripped`, NOT `dropped`, and the caller depends on it: a
        # dropped value is a FINDING, announced per R7, where a stripped one
        # is the human's own policy and is not news on every run.
        [ -z "$_eav" ] || printf 'stripped %s\n' "$_eaname"
      fi
      continue
    fi

    if [ -n "$_eahave" ]; then
      # A value is already here. Judge THAT before touching anything.
      # Guarded for mux_env_plan's `set -e` reason, and this is where it
      # mattered most: bin/mux calls this on every session build and attach,
      # so one dead value stopped the repair for every name after it.
      _eavr=0
      mux_env_validate "$_eaname" "$_eahave" || _eavr=$?
      case $_eavr in
      0) continue ;;            # works: leave it exactly alone
      2) continue ;;            # cannot tell: no finding, so no action
      esac
    fi

    # Either nothing is here (absent or the `-NAME` removal marker), or what
    # is here has been shown to be dead.
    case $_eaverd in
    set|keep)
      if [ "$_ean" -eq 0 ]; then
        if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
          tm set-environment -t "$_eas" \
            "$_eaname" "$_eaval" 2>/dev/null || true
        else
          tm set-environment -g "$_eaname" "$_eaval" 2>/dev/null || true
        fi
      fi
      [ -z "$_eav" ] || printf 'set %s\n' "$_eaname" ;;
    drop|none)
      # Only worth clearing, and worth saying, when something dead is
      # actually here: an absent variable needs no announcement.
      if [ -n "$_eahave" ]; then
        if [ "$_ean" -eq 0 ]; then
          if [ -n "$_eas" ]; then
            tm set-environment -t "$_eas" -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          else
            tm set-environment -g -u "$_eaname" 2>/dev/null || true
          fi
        fi
        [ -z "$_eav" ] || printf 'dropped %s\n' "$_eaname"
      fi ;;
    esac
  done
}

# --- READINESS: has this box's environment arrived yet? --------------------
# THE QUESTION A LATCH NEEDS ANSWERED AFTER A REBOOT. sshd answers seconds
# after boot, so a latch reconnects and its remote `mux resume` rebuilds every
# session while the graphical session is still starting. Measured here: the
# sessions were rebuilt at 23:42:11 and the compositor started at 00:21:29, 39
# minutes later, so six of seven held no display pointers and their panes
# never could, a process environment being fixed at exec.
#
# A RACE AND NOT A TIMING BUG: sshd coming up and the session initialising are
# concurrent, so starting the session earlier changes the odds and not the
# shape. The answer is to WAIT, which means something has to be able to ASK.
#
# DECLARED, NEVER DETECTED, the decision the whole design rests on. mux must
# not learn what Wayland is, and cannot know which pointers a given box will
# ever hold: `envhooks.d` is the SHIPPED tree, identical everywhere, so
# requiring all of it would make any machine that will never have a
# WAYFIRE_SOCKET (another compositor, a headless server, a CI runner) fail
# readiness PERMANENTLY. The caller passes the names it considers load-bearing
# HERE, and with none passed this is free and answers yes.
#
# THE NAME IS THE DECLARATION AND THE HOOK IS THE TEST (the user's design, and
# what keeps this honest): a declared name is checked by running ITS OWN hook
# through the plan above, so there is no second copy of the liveness question.
# Presence is not liveness is why those hooks exist, and a socket left behind
# by the previous graphical session is exactly the value that satisfies a
# presence test while being dead.
#
# `keep` COUNTS AS READY, the three-state contract answered for THIS caller
# rather than by the hook's own certainty. A hook that cannot tell must not
# park a latch in a backoff for ever: the error `et-probe` refuses to make
# where `ssh-probe` makes the opposite call, and the deciding question is
# always what the CALLER does with each state. Waiting costs you the box;
# proceeding costs a pointer that may be stale, today's behaviour.
#
# Sets MUX_ENV_UNREADY (names that are not) and MUX_ENV_UNMANAGED (names
# nothing manages, a typo, reported separately because its failure mode is a
# silent full timeout on every host relatch, so it must be loud when read
# rather than discovered after a reboot).
#
# RETURNS A CODE, so call it `_rc=0; mux_env_ready $names || _rc=$?`. A bare
# call under `set -e` takes the shell down, which is how this very file's plan
# loop was once truncated.
mux_env_ready() {       # <declared name>... -> 0 ready, 1 not, 2 unmanaged
  MUX_ENV_UNREADY=
  MUX_ENV_UNMANAGED=
  # NOTHING DECLARED IS NOT A FAILURE, and the early return also keeps a
  # plan's cost (a systemctl call plus a hook per name) off every box that
  # has not opted in.
  [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || return 0

  _erdm=$(mux_env_names | tr '\n' ' ')
  for _erdn in "$@"; do
    [ -n "$_erdn" ] || continue
    case " $_erdm " in
    *" $_erdn "*) ;;
    *) MUX_ENV_UNMANAGED="$MUX_ENV_UNMANAGED $_erdn" ;;
    esac
  done
  [ -z "$MUX_ENV_UNMANAGED" ] || return 2

  # ONE plan for the whole set: a single systemctl read plus one hook per
  # managed name, where asking per declared name re-runs all of it.
  _erdp=$(mux_env_plan 2>/dev/null)
  for _erdn in "$@"; do
    [ -n "$_erdn" ] || continue
    _erdv=$(printf '%s\n' "$_erdp" \
      | awk -v n="$_erdn" '$2==n{print $1; exit}')
    case $_erdv in
    set|keep) ;;
    *) MUX_ENV_UNREADY="$MUX_ENV_UNREADY $_erdn" ;;
    esac
  done
  [ -z "$MUX_ENV_UNREADY" ] || return 1
  return 0
}
